🚗 Uber Ride Analytics 2024 — Exploratory Data Analysis

A comprehensive exploratory data analysis of 148,770 Uber ride records to uncover insights into demand patterns, revenue drivers, cancellations, operational efficiency, and customer experience.

📌 Project Overview

This project presents a structured end-to-end exploratory data analysis (EDA) of Uber ride operations data for 2024. The objective is to analyze the complete ride lifecycle—from booking request to completion or cancellation—and extract actionable business insights.

The analysis focuses on identifying inefficiencies, understanding demand behavior, and evaluating key performance drivers across the platform.

📊 Dataset Summary
Metric	Value
Total Records	148,770
Features	21
Time Period	Full Year 2024
Geographic Scope	Delhi, India
Vehicle Categories	6
Payment Methods	5
🎯 Objectives

The analysis is designed to address the following business questions:

What proportion of rides are successfully completed?
Which vehicle categories contribute most to revenue?
What are the primary drivers of ride cancellations?
How does demand vary across time (hourly and weekly)?
To what extent does ride distance influence pricing?
Which locations generate the highest booking volumes?
What payment methods are most preferred by customers?
Do rating metrics meaningfully reflect service quality?
🔄 Methodology

The analytical workflow follows a standard data science pipeline:

Data Ingestion → Data Cleaning → Feature Engineering → Exploratory Analysis → Visualization → Insight Generation
🧹 Data Preparation

Key preprocessing steps included:

Handling missing values using context-appropriate strategies (mean, median, or logical defaults)
Standardizing column names for consistency
Converting date and time fields into usable formats
Creating derived features such as hour of booking and day of week
Ensuring no significant data loss during cleaning
📊 Key Insights
🚦 Booking Outcomes
Approximately 66% of rides are completed
Around 25% are cancelled, indicating operational inefficiencies
Nearly 8% of bookings fail due to lack of driver availability, highlighting supply constraints
💰 Revenue Analysis
Auto and Go Mini segments generate the highest revenue due to high usage volume
Premium categories contribute relatively less, indicating limited demand elasticity
Revenue distribution suggests a volume-driven business model
⏱️ Demand Patterns
Peak demand occurs during evening hours (around 6 PM)
Weekend revenue exceeds weekday performance by ~50%
Midday demand remains consistently low
❌ Cancellation Behavior
Driver cancellations are notably high and distributed across multiple reasons
No single dominant cause indicates system-wide inefficiencies
Customer cancellations closely align with delays and availability issues
📍 Geographic Trends
Demand is distributed across multiple pickup locations
No single dominant hotspot, requiring balanced driver allocation
💳 Payment Preferences
UPI is the leading payment method, reflecting regional digital adoption
Cash remains a significant fallback option
A large number of “Not Applicable” entries indicate data quality concerns
⭐ Ratings Analysis
Ratings are heavily concentrated between 4.0 and 5.0
Minimal correlation between ratings and revenue or ride characteristics
Indicates rating inflation and limited discriminatory value
📈 Core Visualizations

The analysis includes a diverse set of visualizations:

Booking status distribution
Revenue by vehicle type
Pairwise feature relationships
Cancellation reason analysis
Demand trends by hour and day
Pickup location analysis
Payment method distribution
Correlation heatmap
🔬 Advanced Analysis

Additional analytical techniques applied:

Multi-dimensional aggregations using groupby and pivot_table
Cross-tabulation with percentage normalization
Outlier detection using the IQR method
Distribution analysis using skewness and kurtosis
Query-based filtering for high-value ride analysis
💡 Strategic Recommendations

Based on the analysis, the following actions are suggested:

1. Improve Driver Retention & Behavior
Introduce incentive and penalty mechanisms to reduce cancellations
Optimize driver allocation and matching algorithms
2. Address Supply Gaps
Increase driver availability during peak hours
Focus on regions with frequent “No Driver Found” cases
3. Optimize Revenue Opportunities
Enhance surge pricing strategies during weekends and peak hours
Strengthen high-performing vehicle categories
4. Enhance Data Quality
Resolve inconsistencies in payment method records
Improve granularity in cancellation reason tracking
5. Strengthen Customer Experience
Reduce wait times (VTAT/CTAT)
Improve reliability in ride fulfillment
